Subject: [PATCH 6/6] zle: leave a host's answer to a terminal query out of the keys

A program that asks the terminal something and ends before it reads the answer, or never reads it,
leaves the host's answer in the terminal's input, and the line editor finds it at its prompt. ZLE
decodes it as keys: an unbound sequence beeps and the rest of the answer is typed into the line. The
reader takes the answer out at the one place the editor takes each byte of a key, before the key
lookup sees it: when the byte is an Escape and the terminal has already delivered the rest of an
answer the host's query broker builds, the whole answer is taken and dropped; anything else is given
back to the key lookup untouched and in order. The bytes given back are still the person's, and the
bridge reports them as the terminal's, not as keys a widget pushed back. A read that waits for the
rest of a key keeps its one KEYTIMEOUT limit however many answers it takes out of the way. Nothing
waits for a byte the terminal has not delivered, so no key a person presses is held back. A widget's
own one-key read, `read -k`, never goes through this read, and `vared`, `select`, a quoted insertion
and a bracketed paste are left alone.

This patch is a derivative work of Zsh and carries the Zsh licence; see shells/zsh/LICENSE.

--- a/Src/Zle/zle.mdd
+++ b/Src/Zle/zle.mdd
@@ -9,7 +9,7 @@
 zle_misc.o zle_move.o zle_params.o zle_refresh.o \
 zle_thingy.o zle_tricky.o zle_utils.o zle_vi.o zle_word.o \
 textobjects.o kr_bridge.o kr_bridge_cbor.o kr_bridge_crypto.o \
-kr_bridge_zle.o"
+kr_bridge_reply.o kr_bridge_zle.o"
 
 headers="zle.h zle_things.h"
 
--- a/Src/Zle/zle_keymap.c
+++ b/Src/Zle/zle_keymap.c
@@ -28,6 +28,9 @@
  */
 
 #include "zle.mdh"
+
+#include "kr_bridge.h"
+#include "kr_bridge_zle.h"
 
 /*
  * Keymap structures:
@@ -1693,7 +1696,11 @@
 static int
 getkeybuf(int w)
 {
-    int c = getbyte((long)w, NULL, 1);
+    /*
+     * A host's answer to a terminal query is no key: when an Escape begins one, the reader takes
+     * the whole answer here and reads on.
+     */
+    int c = kr_zle_key_byte((long)w);
 
     if(c < 0)
 	return EOF;
